---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/siteUrl';
---

<Layout title="Object Selection" description="Select an object in an image, refine the mask locally, and reuse it across Varve without destructive edits.">
  <div class="container-custom">
    <div class="feature-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/features')}>Features</a> / Object Selection
      </nav>

      <h1>Object Selection</h1>
      <p class="feature-intro">
        From object to editable mask. Select an image region on the canvas, refine it with include and exclude prompts, then reuse the mask wherever the design needs it.
      </p>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Select with the canvas</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Choose point prompts or an explicit box-hint mode; create a box by dragging or by tapping its two corners.</li>
          <li>Set include or exclude polarity visibly. Shift remains a temporary exclude shortcut, separate from selection subtraction.</li>
          <li>Tap a prompt marker to remove just that prompt, drag it to move it, or press Backspace for the last one.</li>
          <li>Compare candidate masks before applying; the candidate you reviewed is the one that is committed.</li>
          <li>Preview the result before it becomes part of the document.</li>
          <li>Use replace, add, subtract, or intersect when sending the reviewed candidate to a pixel selection, or apply it as a standalone non-destructive raster mask.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Prompts are not object discovery</h2>
        <p>
          Candidate masks are alternative readings of the same prompt, not separate detected
          instances. Multiple points may describe one object, and Varve keeps disconnected pieces
          and holes instead of silently keeping only the largest component. Use a new prompt session
          when you mean a different object, then combine the accepted selections intentionally.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>A subject estimate with no download</h2>
        <p>
          When you have an obvious subject but no prompts in mind, Selection Sources &gt; Select
          subject proposes foreground regions with no model, no account, and no GPU. Proposals are
          ranked by coverage, centrality, and edge support; the top-ranked one becomes a pixel
          selection immediately, every alternative stays one click away, and All subjects unions
          them. Scores are shown as estimates, never as semantic confidence, and the copy never
          claims to know what an object is.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Selection and trimming are different tools</h2>
        <p>
          Object Selection creates a pixel mask from your prompts. Apply it as an editable mask or
          use it as a temporary pixel selection without changing the artwork. When you only need a
          tighter image frame, Trim to Subject can suggest object bounds separately; it shows the
          detected box for review before applying a non-destructive crop. The bounds workflow is
          not a hidden mask and does not claim semantic recognition.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section">
        <h2>Local by design</h2>
        <p>
          The optional segmentation model pair is downloaded explicitly from Settings &gt; Offline
          Models, verified before use, and run on the device through Varve's worker-backed
          inference path. A model is not loaded at application startup, and image embeddings are
          kept in a bounded session cache rather than stored in your document.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="feature-section note">
        <h2>Set the right expectation</h2>
        <p>
          Promptable segmentation follows your points and box; it is not a guarantee of semantic subject understanding or perfect alpha matting. Hair, glass, smoke, and similar boundaries may need the existing brush or trimap refinement tools. The model-free Select subject estimate is a foreground heuristic, works best on plain or gradient backgrounds, and is weakest on landscape or texture scenes. Review the mask against the final background before export.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="feature-cta">
        <h2>Make the mask part of the design</h2>
        <a href={sitePath('/download')} class="btn-default">Download Varve</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .feature-page { max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 0; }
  .breadcrumb { font-size: 0.875rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .breadcrumb a { color: var(--text-secondary); }
  .feature-page h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.5rem); font-family: var(--font-editorial); margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--text-primary); }
  .feature-intro { font-size: 1.25rem; color: var(--text-secondary); margin-bottom: 3rem; }
  .feature-section { margin-bottom: 3rem; }
  .feature-section h2 { font-size: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; color: var(--text-primary); }
  .feature-section p, .feature-section li { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.7; }
  .feature-section li + li { margin-top: 0.65rem; }
  .note { border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-primary); padding-left: 1rem; }
  .feature-cta { border-top: 1px solid var(--border-default); padding-top: 2rem; }
  .feature-cta h2 { color: var(--text-primary); margin-bottom: 1rem; }
</style>
